= Momentum — Simple =

'''Momentum''' describes how much motion an object has, taking into account both its mass and its velocity. In everyday classical physics, momentum is mass multiplied by velocity.

Momentum also has a direction. Two objects of equal mass moving at equal speeds in opposite directions have opposite momenta.

When objects collide, they exchange momentum. If we include every interacting object and external forces are negligible, total momentum stays the same. This does not mean the objects keep the same speeds or that motion [[Energy|energy]] cannot become heat.

